# The thinking indicator starts where the assistant's first glyph will.
#
# Gabe: "the position of the typing indicator doesnt line up where it should."
#
# WHAT IT SHOULD LINE UP WITH, because that is the actual question. This row is
# the PLACEHOLDER for the assistant's first line of prose -- render_rich_body
# replaces it the moment a token arrives -- so the thing it has to match is
# that line, and anything that does not match is a jump the eye catches at the
# exact moment you are watching the bubble.
#
# It did not match on either axis. The dot's ink ran x=372..383 on the parity
# capture where every line of assistant prose in the same bubble begins at
# x=374, so it hung into the bubble's left padding; and the row was a round 24
# tall where one line of prose is body_text_h(1) = 20, so the placeholder
# bubble was 4px taller than the bubble it turns into.
#
# The left edge is not a nudge, it is a rule the file already knows and this
# row did not use. kBubbleCfgPadX (7) is deliberately kBubblePadX (13) minus
# kLabelInsetX (6), because afterhours adds that 6 back when it draws a LABEL
# into an element's rect and adds nothing for a custom draw. So a TEXT child of
# the bubble puts its first glyph at 13 and a DRAWN child lands at 7, and the
# fix is to give the drawn row the 6 the label path gets for free.
#
# The numbers below are that arithmetic, and the reader can check every one:
#
#   asst_bubble    x=322
#   thinking_row   x=329   = 322 + kBubbleCfgPadX(7)
#   thinking_dot   x=335   = 322 + kBubblePadX(13), the glyph column
#   thinking_row   h=20    = body_text_h(1) = kLinePitch(16) + 2*kBodyPad(2)
#   thinking_dot   w=14    = the halo's own max diameter (5 + 2), so the box
#                            IS the ink and the dot's extent is arithmetic
#                            rather than something measured off a screenshot
#                            (gap #114).
#
# 335 is the glyph column and not an assertion about one: in the SAME fixture
# without the thinking demo, `assert_ui asst_line` reports x=329, and a label's
# first glyph lands kLabelInsetX inside its rect -- 329 + 6 = 335. The dot's
# box begins exactly where a prose line's first glyph begins.
#
# Verified to fail without the fix: restoring `pixels(24.0f)` for the row, the
# 18px dot slot and dropping the row's left padding gives
#
#   assert_ui 'thinking_row': h=20 but got 24              (line 53)
#   assert_ui 'thinking_dot': x=335 but got 329            (line 54)
#
# -- the dot back on the bubble's raw content edge instead of the glyph column,
# and the placeholder four pixels taller than the line it stands in for. (The
# w=14 clause reports only the first mismatch per element, so the 18px slot is
# named by the x it produces rather than by its own number.)
#
# settings: {"window_width":1100,"window_height":760,"open_tabs":["r9"],"active_tab":"r9","theme":"dark"}
# env: HANABI_WIN_W=1100 HANABI_WIN_H=760 HANABI_THINK_DEMO=1

require_thread r9

wait_frames 14

assert_ui asst_bubble x=322
assert_ui thinking_row x=329 h=20
assert_ui thinking_dot x=335 w=14 h=14
